From: Devin <devin@cognition.ai>
Subject: [PATCH tab5] input: keyboard: M5Stack Tab5 Keyboard (A164)
 I2C driver

Tab5's Ext.Port1 keyboard is an STM32F030C8T6 matrix scanner at I2C
0x6d on a dedicated hw GPIO0 (SDA) / GPIO1 (SCL) pair -- a bus of its
own, independent of the bit-banged internal bus on GPIO31/32.

KEY mode is used: the MCU reports raw {press, row, col} events and
the driver does what the MCU's HID layer does with them.  The reason
is the Aa key.  In HID mode the MCU decides upper case itself and
never tells the host, so the host cannot show that state; the only
indicator is the MCU's own LED 0, which shares its brightness
register with the always-lit mode LED 1.  With the keys handled here
the driver knows the state and drives LED 0 alone.

What the keys do is what the firmware does:

  sym   held: the symbol keys give their second character.
  ctrl, alt:  ordinary modifier keys.
  Aa    held: letters are upper case, like a Shift key.
        one tap: the next letter is upper case (LED 0 blinks).
        two taps: upper case stays on until the next tap (LED 0 lit).

Aa is not a key Linux sees.  An upper-case letter is the letter's key
with Shift pressed around it, as a symbol such as '!' is KEY_1 with
Shift.  That Shift belongs to the most recent key: it goes down
before a key that needs it and comes up before one that does not, so
'(' and 'f' typed overlapping are "(f", not "(F".  '1' and '!' are
one keycode on two keys; when both are down the earlier one lets go.

Two things differ from the firmware on purpose.  ctrl and alt reach
every key (the firmware dropped them from the keys that have a sym
character, so Ctrl-[ was a plain '[').  And Aa used as a Shift key --
a letter typed while it is down -- leaves nothing switched on when it
is let go, however short the hold was; the firmware armed the next
letter too.

The key-ready interrupt is hw GPIO50 on gpio bank 1, which the
esp32p4 gpio driver does not wire to an irq_chip, so the driver polls
the chip FIFO at 40 Hz; the MCU queues events until drained.  An idle
poll is one three-byte read.

The unit may be attached after boot, so the driver keeps the input
device registered at all times and tracks presence itself: while the
MCU does not answer it asks for VERSION once a second, and a unit
that replies gets the full configure sequence before polling starts;
eight consecutive I/O failures mean detached, in which case every
held key is released (a gone keyboard reports no releases) and the
one-a-second watch resumes.

An MCU whose power dipped keeps answering I2C but restarts with its
reset values.  KB_MODE cannot show that, because key mode is the
reset value; INT_CFG can (reset 0x07, configured 0x01), and it is the
first byte of the read every poll already does.  On a mismatch the
keys are released and the configure sequence is replayed.

m5stack,led-brightness is how bright LED 0 is when lit, 0..100 (the
MCU ignores a larger value); 0 keeps it dark.

Wire protocol from m5stack M5Tab5-Keyboard-Internal-FW (MIT licensed):

  0x00  INT_CFG    bit0/1/2 = key/hid/char event enables
  0x01  INT_STA    pending event types; write 0 clears + releases INT
  0x02  EVENT_NUM  queued event count; write 0 flushes the FIFO
  0x03  BRIGHTNESS level of the two indicator LEDs, 0..100
  0x10  KB_MODE    0=key, 1=hid, 2=char
  0x11  RGB_MODE   0=bind (firmware owns LEDs), 1=user registers
  0x20  KEY_EVENT  pops one raw event per read:
                   bit7=pressed, bit6-4=row, bit3-0=col; 0xff = empty
  0x60..0x67      LED n at +n*4: [B, G, R, reserved]
  0xfe  VERSION    firmware byte

linux/tests/test-tab5-kbd.py builds this file on the host and runs it
against a model of the MCU.

Signed-off-by: Devin <devin@cognition.ai>
